                <section id="overview">
                    <h2>Overview</h2>
                    <p>The <span class="highlight">Color Image Function Approximation (<b>CIFA</b>)</span> system is an innovative approach to image representation that transforms pixel-based images into mathematical functions. Instead of storing individual pixel values, this method represents images as a collection of polynomial functions that can be evaluated at any point in the image plane.</p>
                    
                    <div class="concept-box">
                        <p><strong>Key Insight:</strong> Natural images often contain smooth color transitions and coherent structures that can be efficiently approximated by mathematical functions, potentially achieving significant compression ratios while preserving visual quality.</p>
                    </div>
                    
                    <p>This approach is particularly valuable for:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Image compression with progressive quality enhancement</li>
                        <li>Resolution-independent image representation</li>
                        <li>Procedural texture generation</li>
                        <li>Neural network input optimization</li>
                        <li>Edge computing applications</li>
                    </ul>
                </section>
                
                <section id="concept">
                    <h2>Core Concept</h2>
                    <p>The system operates on a fundamental observation: color images can be decomposed into multiple brightness layers (slices), and each layer's color distribution can be approximated separately using 2D polynomial functions.</p>
                    
                    <div class="diagram">
                        <h3>Conceptual Flow</h3>
                        <p style="font-family: monospace; color: #666;">
                            Input Image → Luminance Decomposition → Slice-wise Function Fitting → Image Reconstruction
                        </p>
                    </div>
                    
                    <h3>Brightness Slicing</h3>
                    <p>The image is divided into multiple brightness levels based on perceptual luminance:</p>
                    <div class="formula">
                        Luminance = 0.299R + 0.587G + 0.114B
                    </div>
                    
                    <h3>Function Fitting</h3>
                    <p>For each brightness slice, separate polynomial functions are fitted for each color channel (R, G, B). These functions map spatial coordinates (x, y) to color intensity values.</p>
                </section>
                
                <section id="algorithm">
                    <h2>Three-Stage Algorithm</h2>
                    
                    <div class="algorithm-steps">
                        <div class="step">
                            <h3>Stage 1: Luminance Decomposition</h3>
                            <p>The input image is analyzed pixel by pixel to calculate perceptual luminance. Pixels are then grouped into N brightness slices, where N is user-configurable (typically 4-12 levels).</p>
                            <div class="formula">
                                slice_index = floor(luminance × N)
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        
                        <div class="step">
                            <h3>Stage 2: Function Approximation</h3>
                            <p>For each brightness slice, the system:</p>
                            <ol>
                                <li>Samples points based on density parameter</li>
                                <li>Fits 2D polynomial functions for each color channel</li>
                                <li>Stores polynomial coefficients for reconstruction</li>
                            </ol>
                            <p>The polynomial degree is controlled by the complexity parameter.</p>
                        </div>
                        
                        <div class="step">
                            <h3>Stage 3: Image Reconstruction</h3>
                            <p>To reconstruct the image at any resolution:</p>
                            <ol>
                                <li>For each pixel position (x, y), determine which brightness slice(s) contribute</li>
                                <li>Evaluate the appropriate polynomial functions for R, G, B channels</li>
                                <li>Combine results and clip to valid color range [0, 255]</li>
                            </ol>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </section>
                
                <section id="mathematics">
                    <h2>Mathematical Foundation</h2>
                    
                    <h3>Polynomial Basis Functions</h3>
                    <p>The system uses 2D polynomials of degree D:</p>
                    <div class="formula">
                        P(x, y) = Σᵢⱼ aᵢⱼ × xⁱ × yʲ
                    </div>
                    <p>where i + j ≤ D, and aᵢⱼ are the polynomial coefficients.</p>
                    
                    <h3>Least Squares Fitting</h3>
                    <p>Given sample points (xₖ, yₖ) with color values cₖ, we solve:</p>
                    <div class="formula">
                        minimize Σₖ [P(xₖ, yₖ) - cₖ]²
                    </div>
                    
                    <p>This is solved using the normal equations:</p>
                    <div class="formula">
                        AᵀA x = Aᵀb
                    </div>
                    
                    <h3>Error Metrics</h3>
                    <p>Reconstruction quality is measured using:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Mean Squared Error (MSE):</strong> Average squared difference per pixel</li>
                        <li><strong>Peak Signal-to-Noise Ratio (PSNR):</strong> Logarithmic quality measure</li>
                        <li><strong>Maximum Error:</strong> Worst-case pixel difference</li>
                    </ul>
                </section>
                
